+package cert
+
+import "time"
+
+// ClockSkewTolerance widens the validity window applied by Expired() to
+// absorb local-clock skew. With a positive value the validator considers
+// the cert valid from (NotBefore - tolerance) to (NotAfter + tolerance).
+// Default zero preserves upstream behavior.
+//
+// Set this from the agent's startup once a configured value is known.
+// Race-free in practice: only ever assigned during agent boot before any
+// CAPool/Certificate is constructed; reads are concurrent and unsynchronized
+// because the value is constant for the lifetime of the process.
+//
+// Why this exists (hopssh patch 25): Nebula's strict cert validation
+// rejects every handshake when the local clock has drifted beyond the
+// 24h cert window — UTM-suspended VMs, sleepy laptops, boards without
+// RTC. Tailscale doesn't have this problem because WireGuard uses
+// static keys with no time-bounded credential between peers. We can't
+// change Nebula's PKI but we can soften the validity check by a small
+// configurable window. A 1h tolerance gives a comfortable margin on
+// 24h certs without meaningfully weakening the security property.
+var ClockSkewTolerance time.Duration
